Before trusting any of these numbers, it's worth a glance at whether the sampler actually did its job: the largest Rhat is 1.00 (comfortably under the 1.01 threshold that indicates the chains agree with each other), the smallest bulk and tail effective sample sizes are 2259 (far above the 400 we'd want as a minimum), and there are 0 divergent transitions. That's what a trustworthy fit looks like: cheap to check, and worth checking every single time, not just when something looks off.

### parsnip






::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(parsnip)
library(broom)

# The same model specified through parsnip, fitted by ordinary least squares
parsnip_model <- linear_reg() %>%
  set_engine("lm") %>%
  fit(Gfap_Intensity ~ Condition, data = cell_data_sub)

# Coefficients with 95% confidence intervals
tidy(parsnip_model, conf.int = TRUE)
